1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,243
Pages, stylesheets, scripts and JSON responses are text, and text compresses well.

2
00:00:06,243 --> 00:00:11,069
The Response Compression module makes a tenant, one site of an Orchard Core

3
00:00:11,069 --> 00:00:13,571
application, compress its responses.

4
00:00:13,571 --> 00:00:18,040
You will see how to enable it, what it registers, and when to use it.

5
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:27,345
A client lists the formats it can unpack in the Accept-Encoding request header.

6
00:00:27,345 --> 00:00:32,216
When the server compresses a response, it names the format in the Content-Encoding

7
00:00:32,216 --> 00:00:35,260
response header, and the client unpacks the body.

8
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:41,551
Text such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript, JSON and XML benefits from compression.

9
00:00:41,551 --> 00:00:47,640
Files that are already compressed, such as PNG images, gain little, and compressing

10
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:51,060
them again costs the server processing time.

11
00:00:55,312 --> 00:00:58,186
In the admin menu, open Tools, then Features.

12
00:00:58,186 --> 00:01:00,553
The page lists features by category.

13
00:01:00,553 --> 00:01:05,342
Let's search for Response Compression, which is in the Infrastructure category.

14
00:01:05,819 --> 00:01:10,932
The row shows the name of the feature, its identifier, and a Disabled badge.

15
00:01:10,932 --> 00:01:12,179
Select Enable.

16
00:01:12,654 --> 00:01:16,628
The page confirms that the feature Response Compression was enabled.

17
00:01:16,628 --> 00:01:22,374
The tenant is rebuilt on its next request, and its pipeline then includes the compression middleware.

18
00:01:22,858 --> 00:01:25,931
The row shows an Enabled badge and a Disable button.

19
00:01:25,931 --> 00:01:30,328
The feature belongs to this tenant, so each tenant that needs compression has

20
00:01:30,328 --> 00:01:32,288
the feature enabled separately.

21
00:01:36,488 --> 00:01:41,308
A setup or deployment recipe enables the feature with the Feature step.

22
00:01:41,308 --> 00:01:46,878
List the identifier under enable, and the recipe turns the feature on when it runs.

23
00:01:51,078 --> 00:01:55,010
The module registers its work in one Startup class.

24
00:01:55,010 --> 00:02:01,355
In ConfigureServices, it calls AddResponseCompression and sets EnableForHttps to true.

25
00:02:01,355 --> 00:02:06,942
It lists no providers, so the defaults of ASP.NET Core apply, which include

26
00:02:06,942 --> 00:02:09,218
Zstandard, Brotli and gzip.

27
00:02:09,968 --> 00:02:12,293
Configure calls UseResponseCompression.

28
00:02:12,293 --> 00:02:16,168
The Order is minus five, below the default of zero, so the middleware

29
00:02:16,168 --> 00:02:20,300
sits early in the tenant pipeline, ahead of the middleware of modules that

30
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:21,798
keep the default order.

31
00:02:22,548 --> 00:02:29,710
The middleware compresses a response when the request's Accept-Encoding names a supported format,

32
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:33,394
and the response's content type is on its list.

33
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:40,138
By default, the list includes HTML, CSS, JavaScript, JSON, XML, plain text and WebAssembly.

34
00:02:40,888 --> 00:02:45,367
In the default web project, UseStaticFiles runs before UseOrchardCore.

35
00:02:45,367 --> 00:02:50,553
Files it serves from the project's wwwroot folder never reach the tenant pipeline,

36
00:02:50,553 --> 00:02:53,205
so this feature does not compress them.

37
00:02:53,205 --> 00:02:58,508
Files of modules and themes are served inside the pipeline, so it can compress them.

38
00:03:02,708 --> 00:03:08,946
To check the result, send a request that accepts one format, and print the response headers.

39
00:03:08,946 --> 00:03:15,501
Accept-Encoding gzip returns Content-Encoding gzip, and Accept-Encoding br, the name of Brotli in

40
00:03:15,501 --> 00:03:18,428
the header, returns Content-Encoding br.

41
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:24,231
A request without an Accept-Encoding header gets an uncompressed response.

42
00:03:24,231 --> 00:03:31,557
When a client lists several formats without weights, the middleware prefers Zstandard, then Brotli, then gzip.

43
00:03:31,557 --> 00:03:35,598
A web server in front of the application can compress too.

44
00:03:39,798 --> 00:03:45,102
Compression is usually more efficient in a reverse proxy or web server, such

45
00:03:45,102 --> 00:03:46,977
as IIS, Nginx or a CDN.

46
00:03:46,977 --> 00:03:53,768
Use the module when no such layer compresses, for example when the application is exposed directly.

47
00:03:54,518 --> 00:04:00,058
The module sets EnableForHttps to true, so HTTPS responses are compressed too.

48
00:04:00,058 --> 00:04:07,377
Compressing dynamically generated, secured responses can expose an application to attacks such as BREACH.

49
00:04:07,377 --> 00:04:10,938
Enable the feature only if you accept that risk.

50
00:04:12,938 --> 00:04:18,305
The Response Compression module adds ASP.NET Core response compression to a tenant with

51
00:04:18,305 --> 00:04:21,998
one feature, so weigh the HTTPS risk before you enable it.

52
00:04:21,998 --> 00:04:23,498
Thanks for watching.

